/*****************************************************************************
    jitXand -- THE genKantParse SKETCH'S CONJUNCTION, MEASURED BOTH ENGINES.
    2026-08-08.

    THE SKETCH under discussion writes a sequence rule's body as

        if sukcess = t1() AND t2() AND t3();

    That spelling has TWO requirements, and only one of them is about the JIT:

      (1) SHORT-CIRCUIT, which for a parser is a SEMANTIC requirement and not a
          style preference. If t1() fails, t2() must NOT run -- a parse term
          consumes input, so an eagerly-evaluated right arm advances the mark
          past text the rule never matched. CLAIM KANT-34 already records that
          `||` and `&&` evaluate BOTH arms interpreted, and records the reason
          as STRUCTURAL: an operateMethod receives operands the runtime has
          already evaluated, so there is no point at which opAND could decline.
          THIS FIXTURE ASKS WHETHER THE JITTED PATH INHERITS THAT or diverges.

      (2) EMISSION. opAND is on jit.md S2.1's NOT-GATED list and has no
          jitDegrade call, so the predicted behaviour is silent emit-time
          interpretation at degrade count 0 -- the H4 failure family, where the
          counter that is supposed to catch a fallback cannot see this one.

    xaTicksR IS THE DISCRIMINATOR AND IT IS A COUNT (H4). The left arm is made
    to FAIL, so a short-circuiting AND leaves the right arm's counter at 0 and
    an eager one bumps it. An absence-of-output check could not tell those
    apart from a right arm that ran and printed nothing.

    DIAGNOSTIC, fire 1 (xaLeftOK = 0, so the left arm returns 0):
        ticksL 1  ticksR 0   SHORT-CIRCUIT -- the sketch is safe as written
        ticksL 1  ticksR 1   EAGER -- the sketch consumes input on a failed term
    and across the two fires:
        ticksL 1 -> 2        the calls are emitted and run per fire
        ticksL 1 -> 1        the conjunction ran at EMIT time, nothing emitted

    Fire 2 flips xaLeftOK to 1 AFTER emission with no recompile, so the two
    fires are required to differ; a pair that agreed would prove nothing.

    ORDER (bear-trap #25): jitted half FIRST, oracle last.
    SENTINEL AT THE FOOT (H2).
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);

search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests bcOPs Utilities list;

register(JitXand);
define
    xaLeftOK;
    xaTicksL;
    xaTicksR;
    xaOut;
    xaLeft code={
        xaTicksL = xaTicksL + 1;
        return xaLeftOK;
        };
    xaRight code={
        xaTicksR = xaTicksR + 1;
        return 1;
        };
    xaDriver code={
        xaOut = xaLeft() AND xaRight();
        };
    ;

xaLeftOK = 0;
xaTicksL = 0;
xaTicksR = 0;
xaOut    = 9;
print "XA fire 1 setup : xaLeftOK =" xaLeftOK " (left arm FAILS)":;
testing(xaDriver);
print "XA fire 1 result: xaOut =" xaOut " ticksL =" xaTicksL " ticksR =" xaTicksR:;
print "XA fire 1 verdict: ticksR 0 = short-circuit, 1 = eager":;

xaLeftOK = 1;
print "XA fire 2 setup : xaLeftOK =" xaLeftOK " (left arm SUCCEEDS, recompile: NONE)":;
jitRefire();
print "XA fire 2 result: xaOut =" xaOut " ticksL =" xaTicksL " ticksR =" xaTicksR:;
print "XA fire 2 verdict: ticksL 2 = emitted per fire, 1 = ran at emit time":;

xaLeftOK = 0;
xaTicksL = 0;
xaTicksR = 0;
xaOut    = 9;
xaDriver();
print "XA interpreted  : xaOut =" xaOut " ticksL =" xaTicksL " ticksR =" xaTicksR:;

print "XA SENTINEL -- run reached the end":;
stop();
